1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,826
Three hundred seventy-one thousand.

2
00:00:01,846 --> 00:00:05,017
That is how many German prisoners of war

3
00:00:05,037 --> 00:00:10,110
the United States was holding in May 1945.

4
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:12,254
Most of them crossed America the same way:

5
00:00:12,274 --> 00:00:14,150
by train, looking out of a window.

6
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,480
They had been told what they would find.

7
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,480
American cities in ruins.

8
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:23,980
A country starving and afraid.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,827
In 1944, an Iowa newspaper ran a headline

10
00:00:27,847 --> 00:00:29,933
that said German prisoners believed New

11
00:00:29,953 --> 00:00:32,060
York was being bombed every day.

12
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:35,013
Then the trains rolled west,

13
00:00:35,033 --> 00:00:37,706
mile after mile, and the men at the

14
00:00:37,726 --> 00:00:39,052
windows saw something else.

15
00:00:39,072 --> 00:00:41,408
This is the documented story of what

16
00:00:41,428 --> 00:00:43,091
roughly three hundred seventy thousand

17
00:00:43,111 --> 00:00:45,110
German prisoners saw from American trains,

18
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:47,150
and what it did to them.

19
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,094
It started in North Africa.

20
00:00:51,114 --> 00:00:54,900
In May 1943, about two hundred

21
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:56,633
seventy-five thousand Axis soldiers

22
00:00:56,653 --> 00:00:57,933
surrendered in Tunisia.

23
00:00:57,953 --> 00:01:00,533
Britain could not hold them all.

24
00:01:00,553 --> 00:01:03,567
The year before, the United States had

25
00:01:03,587 --> 00:01:06,167
reluctantly agreed to take fifty thousand

26
00:01:06,187 --> 00:01:06,600
prisoners.

27
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:09,220
Now they came by the shipload.

28
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,233
Most crossed the Atlantic in Liberty ships

29
00:01:13,253 --> 00:01:15,936
that would otherwise have sailed home empty.

30
00:01:15,956 --> 00:01:18,638
Some came on great ocean liners painted

31
00:01:18,658 --> 00:01:19,411
wartime gray.

32
00:01:19,431 --> 00:01:22,499
A single trip on the Queen Mary could

33
00:01:22,519 --> 00:01:24,450
carry about fifteen thousand prisoners.

34
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,116
The convoys zigzagged to dodge U-boats.

35
00:01:28,136 --> 00:01:30,841
The prisoners knew the danger better than

36
00:01:30,861 --> 00:01:31,230
anyone:

37
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:33,955
the submarines out there were their own.

38
00:01:33,975 --> 00:01:36,700
But the meals on board were big.

39
00:01:38,425 --> 00:01:40,371
The ships landed at Norfolk,

40
00:01:40,391 --> 00:01:43,122
Virginia, at New York, and at Boston.

41
00:01:43,142 --> 00:01:46,267
At the peak, as many as thirty thousand

42
00:01:46,287 --> 00:01:48,233
prisoners a month were arriving.

43
00:01:48,253 --> 00:01:51,035
Between August and November 1944 alone,

44
00:01:51,055 --> 00:01:54,025
more than a hundred and ten thousand came through.

45
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:57,624
Günter Gräwe was eighteen when the

46
00:01:57,644 --> 00:02:00,358
Americans captured him in a field hospital

47
00:02:00,378 --> 00:02:01,139
in Normandy.

48
00:02:01,159 --> 00:02:05,058
In August 1944 he crossed on the Queen

49
00:02:05,078 --> 00:02:07,548
Mary with a few thousand other prisoners.

50
00:02:07,568 --> 00:02:10,038
He remembered the voyage as strangely luxurious.

51
00:02:10,058 --> 00:02:12,172
Then came a long train ride,

52
00:02:12,192 --> 00:02:15,017
all the way across the country to Fort

53
00:02:15,037 --> 00:02:16,460
Lewis, in Washington State.

54
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:22,164
On January second, 1944, the liner Empress

55
00:02:22,184 --> 00:02:24,762
of Scotland unloaded its prisoners at Norfolk.

56
00:02:24,782 --> 00:02:27,732
One of them was a German soldier named

57
00:02:27,752 --> 00:02:28,475
Reinhold Pabel.

58
00:02:28,495 --> 00:02:30,702
The men were deloused, lined up,

59
00:02:30,722 --> 00:02:32,950
and marched to the railway station.

60
00:02:34,275 --> 00:02:38,304
Many of them had ridden to war in freight cars.

61
00:02:38,324 --> 00:02:41,543
The train at Norfolk was made of passenger

62
00:02:41,563 --> 00:02:43,162
coaches with upholstered seats.

63
00:02:43,182 --> 00:02:45,592
Some prisoners later remembered them as

64
00:02:45,612 --> 00:02:46,401
Pullman cars.

65
00:02:46,421 --> 00:02:48,426
These modern coaches, Pabel wrote,

66
00:02:48,446 --> 00:02:50,875
were a pleasant surprise to everyone.

67
00:02:52,175 --> 00:02:55,061
Then a Black porter came down the aisle

68
00:02:55,081 --> 00:02:56,514
with coffee and sandwiches.

69
00:02:56,534 --> 00:02:59,057
Pabel never forgot the way he offered

70
00:02:59,077 --> 00:02:59,420
them:

71
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:01,599
politely, as though the prisoners were

72
00:03:01,619 --> 00:03:02,326
human beings.

73
00:03:02,346 --> 00:03:04,869
Most of us, he wrote, forgot our

74
00:03:04,889 --> 00:03:05,689
anti-American feelings.

75
00:03:07,375 --> 00:03:08,755
The comfort had limits.

76
00:03:08,775 --> 00:03:11,905
A guard stood at each end of the coach.

77
00:03:11,925 --> 00:03:14,355
A prisoner who needed the washroom raised

78
00:03:14,375 --> 00:03:17,175
his hand and was escorted there and back.

79
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:21,172
The Army told its guards to be correct,

80
00:03:21,192 --> 00:03:21,858
never friendly.

81
00:03:21,878 --> 00:03:23,572
One instruction warned against soft,

82
00:03:23,592 --> 00:03:25,629
sticky sentiment, against what it called

83
00:03:25,649 --> 00:03:26,314
marshmallow friendliness.

84
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:28,371
A model guard put it simply:

85
00:03:28,391 --> 00:03:31,456
you don't have to be cruel to these men,

86
00:03:31,476 --> 00:03:34,542
but you have to show them they won't get

87
00:03:34,562 --> 00:03:35,590
away with anything.

88
00:03:37,275 --> 00:03:39,835
And then there was the window.

89
00:03:39,855 --> 00:03:42,415
Pabel's train ran west through Virginia

90
00:03:42,435 --> 00:03:43,275
and Kentucky.

91
00:03:43,295 --> 00:03:45,855
We pressed our noses against the

92
00:03:45,875 --> 00:03:47,145
windowpanes, he wrote.

93
00:03:47,165 --> 00:03:50,585
The first thing that struck them was the

94
00:03:50,605 --> 00:03:51,875
number of automobiles.

95
00:03:51,895 --> 00:03:53,165
They were everywhere.

96
00:03:53,185 --> 00:03:55,745
So were the wooden frame houses,

97
00:03:55,765 --> 00:03:57,055
especially in Kentucky.

98
00:03:58,475 --> 00:04:01,573
The prisoners had come from a continent at war.

99
00:04:01,593 --> 00:04:04,691
Out here, the war was nowhere to be seen.

100
00:04:04,711 --> 00:04:07,463
The sheer size of the country amazed them,

101
00:04:07,483 --> 00:04:09,196
and so did its prosperity,

102
00:04:09,216 --> 00:04:10,255
untouched by bombs.

103
00:04:11,650 --> 00:04:14,255
Some of them saw Black Americans for the

104
00:04:14,275 --> 00:04:14,911
first time.

105
00:04:14,931 --> 00:04:17,536
Many saw paper plates for the first time.

106
00:04:17,556 --> 00:04:20,489
After a meal, they asked the guards how to

107
00:04:20,509 --> 00:04:23,442
wash them, and were told to throw them out

108
00:04:23,462 --> 00:04:24,427
of the window.

109
00:04:24,447 --> 00:04:27,052
One museum exhibit says the trains ran the

110
00:04:27,072 --> 00:04:29,349
same routes so often that the embankments

111
00:04:29,369 --> 00:04:31,317
filled with paper plates and napkins,

112
00:04:31,337 --> 00:04:32,650
like banks of snow.

113
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:36,519
Not every journey was comfortable.

114
00:04:36,539 --> 00:04:39,982
Hans Galhard rode to a camp in Colorado.

115
00:04:40,002 --> 00:04:42,146
He called the trip brutalizing.

116
00:04:42,166 --> 00:04:44,744
It lasted about a hundred hours.

117
00:04:44,764 --> 00:04:48,206
The men slept sitting up, and in each

118
00:04:48,226 --> 00:04:50,804
coach two guards carried automatic pistols

119
00:04:50,824 --> 00:04:52,555
and two carried clubs.

120
00:04:53,875 --> 00:04:56,689
A few prisoners later made worse claims,

121
00:04:56,709 --> 00:04:59,522
of crowded freight cars and spoiled food.

122
00:04:59,542 --> 00:05:01,546
Historian Arnold Krammer found such

123
00:05:01,566 --> 00:05:03,995
stories extremely rare, and never proven.

124
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:08,616
At every stop, people came to look.

125
00:05:08,636 --> 00:05:11,090
William Oberdieck, captured in Africa in

126
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:14,614
1943, rode a train that went slowly

127
00:05:14,634 --> 00:05:16,778
chugging across the country to Kansas.

128
00:05:16,798 --> 00:05:18,582
Crowds gathered on the platforms.

129
00:05:18,602 --> 00:05:22,189
They came, he said, to see if we had horns

130
00:05:22,209 --> 00:05:23,009
or something.

131
00:05:24,225 --> 00:05:25,689
Not everyone was curious.

132
00:05:25,709 --> 00:05:28,658
The head of the Army's prisoner of war

133
00:05:28,678 --> 00:05:31,256
division received hundreds of letters a week.

134
00:05:31,276 --> 00:05:33,483
One suggested putting the prisoners in

135
00:05:33,503 --> 00:05:36,452
Death Valley with a side of beef and

136
00:05:36,472 --> 00:05:37,585
letting them starve.

137
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:41,937
At Mexia, in Texas, three thousand two

138
00:05:41,957 --> 00:05:44,172
hundred fifty prisoners arrived at once,

139
00:05:44,192 --> 00:05:47,151
in a town of about six thousand people.

140
00:05:47,171 --> 00:05:49,758
Still wearing their desert khaki and short

141
00:05:49,778 --> 00:05:51,993
pants, they marched to the camp.

142
00:05:52,013 --> 00:05:54,228
One resident remembered that the line

143
00:05:54,248 --> 00:05:56,110
stretched the full three miles.

144
00:05:57,450 --> 00:06:00,749
In Kansas, Oberdieck was taken to a dining hall.

145
00:06:00,769 --> 00:06:03,331
He looked at the tables and thought:

146
00:06:03,351 --> 00:06:05,544
are they going to shoot us?

147
00:06:05,564 --> 00:06:07,388
Is this our last meal?

148
00:06:07,408 --> 00:06:09,990
The tables were set with real dinnerware.

149
00:06:11,575 --> 00:06:14,338
There were other men on those trains.

150
00:06:14,358 --> 00:06:17,490
In 1944, Corporal Rupert Trimmingham and

151
00:06:17,510 --> 00:06:19,402
eight other Black soldiers were travelling

152
00:06:19,422 --> 00:06:21,952
from Louisiana to an Army post in Arizona.

153
00:06:21,972 --> 00:06:23,864
At a lunchroom on the way,

154
00:06:23,884 --> 00:06:26,413
they were sent to eat in the kitchen,

155
00:06:26,433 --> 00:06:28,325
while about two dozen German prisoners,

156
00:06:28,345 --> 00:06:30,895
with their guards, were served at the tables.

157
00:06:32,250 --> 00:06:34,979
Trimmingham wrote to the Army magazine Yank.

158
00:06:34,999 --> 00:06:36,942
He asked a simple question:

159
00:06:36,962 --> 00:06:40,084
why are they treated better than we are?

160
00:06:40,104 --> 00:06:41,655
He was not alone.

161
00:06:41,675 --> 00:06:43,618
The historian John Hope Franklin

162
00:06:43,638 --> 00:06:45,974
remembered Black soldiers packed into one

163
00:06:45,994 --> 00:06:48,330
coach, while German prisoners rode with

164
00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:49,116
more room.

165
00:06:49,136 --> 00:06:51,865
The prisoners, he said, found it the

166
00:06:51,885 --> 00:06:52,685
funniest thing.

167
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:56,200
The Army would stay segregated until

168
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:58,133
President Truman ordered it ended,

169
00:06:58,153 --> 00:07:00,075
in 1948.

170
00:07:01,175 --> 00:07:03,499
In the camps, the prisoners worked on

171
00:07:03,519 --> 00:07:06,177
farms and in forests, for up to eighty

172
00:07:06,197 --> 00:07:08,186
cents a day in canteen coupons.

173
00:07:08,206 --> 00:07:11,900
Most were still in America in 1946.

174
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:14,861
Decades later, Günter Gräwe went back to

175
00:07:14,881 --> 00:07:16,815
Fort Lewis to say thank you.

176
00:07:16,835 --> 00:07:18,770
We were always treated with fairness,

177
00:07:18,790 --> 00:07:21,051
he wrote, and we got sufficient food,

178
00:07:21,071 --> 00:07:22,700
and eighty cents a day.

179
00:07:23,975 --> 00:07:26,221
Reinhold Pabel, the prisoner from the

180
00:07:26,241 --> 00:07:28,487
Norfolk train, never forgot the porter

181
00:07:28,507 --> 00:07:29,620
with the coffee.

182
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:31,986
In September 1945 he escaped,

183
00:07:32,006 --> 00:07:34,405
and lived in Chicago under another name

184
00:07:34,425 --> 00:07:35,788
for almost eight years.

185
00:07:35,808 --> 00:07:38,898
That is another story, and we have told it

186
00:07:38,918 --> 00:07:39,955
on this channel.

187
00:07:41,650 --> 00:07:43,842
If someone in your family rode one of

188
00:07:43,862 --> 00:07:45,225
those trains, as a prisoner,

189
00:07:45,245 --> 00:07:48,010
a guard, or a passenger, tell us in the comments.

190
00:07:49,375 --> 00:07:51,430
Three hundred seventy thousand men were

191
00:07:51,450 --> 00:07:53,159
told America was in ruins.

192
00:07:53,179 --> 00:07:55,926
They found out the truth the same way,

193
00:07:55,946 --> 00:07:57,675
one window at a time.

